== Module (chicken random)

This module provides access to entropy (as provided by the operating
system) and a pseudo random number generator.

On UNIX systems entropy is by default obtained from {{/dev/urandom}}.
On Linux, the {{getrandom(2)}} system call is used instead, if available.
On OpenBSD it is {{arc4random_buf(3)}}, on Windows {{RtlGenRandom}}.

The pseudo random number generator is an implementation of the
[[https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Well_equidistributed_long-period_linear|WELL]] algorithm.
At startup it is seeded from the entropy source, so every process
draws a different sequence, unless the program was started with the
{{-:R}} runtime option (see [[Using the compiler]]), in which case it
draws the same sequence on every run.

==== set-pseudo-random-seed!

<procedure>(set-pseudo-random-seed! SEED [SIZE])</procedure>

Set seed for the PRNG from the at most {{SIZE}} bytes of {{SEED}},
which should be a bytevector containing random data.
If {{SIZE}} is not given, it defaults to the size of {{SEED}}.

The same seed always gives the same sequence of pseudo random numbers,
and every byte of it counts: seeds that differ in any of their first
{{SIZE}} bytes, or only in their length, give different sequences.
Bytes past {{SIZE}} are never read.  Any seed is acceptable, including
an empty or all-zero one, but a seed shorter than the state of the
PRNG (the size of the bytevector returned by {{(random-bytes)}}) can
carry no more randomness than it holds.


==== pseudo-random-integer

<procedure>(pseudo-random-integer RANGE [STATE])</procedure>

Returns a uniformly distributed pseudo random integer between 0 and
{{RANGE-1}}, which may be a big integer.  It is drawn from the state
object {{STATE}} if given, and from the default generator otherwise.


==== pseudo-random-real

<procedure>(pseudo-random-real [STATE])</procedure>

Returns a uniformly distributed pseudo-random inexact number between
0 and 1, drawn from the state object {{STATE}} if given, and from the
default generator otherwise.  The result is the uniformly chosen real
number rounded to the nearest flonum, so it can be 1.0 (with a
probability of about 2^-54) and, in principle, 0.0.


=== State objects

The procedures above draw from one default generator shared by the
whole program.  A ''state object'' is an independent generator with the
same algorithm: drawing from it leaves the default generator, and every
other state object, unchanged.  This lets a program run several
reproducible streams side by side, save and replay a stream, or give
each of several simulations its own.

==== make-pseudo-random-state

<procedure>(make-pseudo-random-state [SEED])</procedure>

Returns a new state object seeded from {{SEED}}, a bytevector or an
exact nonnegative integer.  A bytevector is used as by
{{set-pseudo-random-seed!}}, so {{(make-pseudo-random-state SEED)}}
draws the same sequence that the default generator draws after
{{(set-pseudo-random-seed! SEED)}}.  An integer stands for its bytes in
little-endian order, at least one: {{17}} seeds like
{{(bytevector 17)}}.  Without {{SEED}}, the state is seeded from the
entropy source.

==== pseudo-random-state?

<procedure>(pseudo-random-state? X)</procedure>

Returns {{#t}} if {{X}} is a state object, {{#f}} otherwise.

==== pseudo-random-state-copy

<procedure>(pseudo-random-state-copy STATE)</procedure>

Returns a new state object in the same state as {{STATE}}.  The copy
draws the same sequence as {{STATE}} draws from now on, and drawing
from either does not advance the other.

==== pseudo-random-state-split

<procedure>(pseudo-random-state-split [STATE])</procedure>

Returns a new state object seeded from numbers drawn from {{STATE}},
or from the default generator if {{STATE}} is not given or {{#f}}.  This
advances {{STATE}}.  The new state's sequence is determined by
{{STATE}}'s state, so splitting a copy gives the same new state, but it
is neither a continuation of {{STATE}}'s sequence nor linearly related
to it: the numbers are passed through a nonlinear mixing function first.


==== random-bytes

<procedure>(random-bytes [BUF [SIZE]])</procedure>

Returns random bytes from the available entropy source. If {{BUF}} is
given, it should be a string or byte vector which will be filled with up to
{{SIZE}} random bytes. {{SIZE}} defaults to the size of the {{BUF}}
argument. If no arguments are given, {{random-bytes}} returns a
freshly allocated byte vector of sufficient size to be used as a seed
for {{set-pseudo-random-seed!}}.


